# Every tracked file under these trees is a build product lup owns: compiled
# from typed source, proven by the `.lup-ownership.json` digest manifest beside
# it, and regenerated rather than hand-merged. Merging their text is meaningless
# — two branches always disagree on source_digest and on every sha256 a
# regeneration touched — and for one file it is worse than meaningless. The
# compiled hook dispatcher, `plugins/lup/hooks/scripts/policy.py`, is executed
# by the runtime rather than read: conflict markers in it leave a script that
# will not parse, and the permission boundary answers every Bash and Edit call
# in that session by refusing, so the merge cannot even be aborted from inside.
#
# The `lup-ownership` driver keeps one side so the merge resolves, and the
# settle guard `git hooks install` arms at post-merge and post-commit runs
# `lup-devtools git settle`, which regenerates the tree against the merged
# source and folds what that writes into the merge commit.
#
# The driver is per-clone git config, which no repository can ship. Register it
# with `lup-devtools dev merge-driver`; without it git falls back to the text
# merge and these files conflict as they did before. A merge performed on a
# forge's server rather than in a clone reads no git config either, so nothing
# declared here reaches it.
.claude/** merge=lup-ownership
.codex/** merge=lup-ownership
.agents/** merge=lup-ownership
packages/lup/src/lup/web/bundles/** merge=lup-ownership
#
# The committed half of the ledger is the other case, and the opposite one:
# append-only lines with unique ids, so two branches that both appended are
# both right, and git's own `union` driver — needing no per-clone registration
# — keeps every line of each side, with a read folding any duplicate by id.
ledger/journal.jsonl merge=union
